  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <h2 id="renames-and-retired-syntax">Renames and retired syntax</h2>
  <p>
    The full table is on the <a href={`${href('actions-foundations')}#renames-and-retired-syntax`}>Foundations page</a>.
    The five that cost the most marks:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Starter workflows</strong> = <strong>workflow templates</strong>.</li>
    <li><code>::set-output</code> and <code>::save-state</code> → <code>$GITHUB_OUTPUT</code> and <code>$GITHUB_STATE</code>; <code>::set-env</code> and <code>::add-path</code> → <code>$GITHUB_ENV</code> and <code>$GITHUB_PATH</code>.</li>
    <li><code>upload-artifact@v3</code> → <code>@v7</code> (and <code>download-artifact@v8</code>); v4 and later are immutable per name.</li>
    <li><code>runs.using: node16</code> / <code>node20</code> → <code>node24</code>.</li>
    <li>Organisation "required workflows" → a <strong>ruleset</strong> with <strong>Require workflows to pass before merging</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <h2 id="where-the-study-guide-and-the-docs-disagree">Where the study guide, Learn and the docs disagree</h2>
  <p>
    When the official sources disagree, these notes follow the live documentation. Here is every discrepancy found while
    writing them, checked on {SITE.checked}, so neither version throws you.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Source says</th><th>The documentation and product say</th><th>What to remember</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Study guide: "starter workflows"</td><td>"Workflow templates", still stored in <code>actions/starter-workflows</code></td><td>Same feature, two names</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Study guide: YAML anchors, aliases <strong>and merge <code>&lt;&lt;</code></strong></td><td>Docs describe only <code>&amp;</code> anchors and <code>*</code> aliases (supported since September 2025); community reports say <code>&lt;&lt;</code> is still rejected</td><td>Expand merged mappings when reading; do not rely on <code>&lt;&lt;</code> in a workflow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Study guide: "immutable actions rollout on hosted runners" and "immutable actions enforcement"</td><td>Immutable actions were removed from GitHub's roadmap on 11 December 2025. What shipped: immutable releases (October 2025), the SHA pinning policy (August 2025) and workflow lockfiles (GA 28 October 2026)</td><td>"Make the version unchangeable" = full SHA, lockfile or immutable release</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Study guide: "Ubuntu 20.04 deprecation, Windows Server 2025 migration for windows-latest"</td><td>Both finished in 2025. The live change is <code>ubuntu-latest</code> moving to Ubuntu 26.04 (19 October – 19 November 2026)</td><td>The lesson is the same: <code>-latest</code> labels move; pin when you need reproducibility</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Study guide: "required reviewers for unverified actions"</td><td>No single setting does this</td><td>Allowed-actions policy (verified creators plus patterns) and CODEOWNERS review of workflow changes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Study guide: retention policies "(logs, artifacts, workflow runs)" via REST</td><td>One <code>artifact-and-log-retention</code> setting per enterprise, organisation and repository; runs, logs and artifacts can also be deleted individually</td><td>Lower levels can only shorten the retention</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Study guide: "Microsoft associate, expert and specialty certifications expire annually"</td><td>The GH-200 certification page: GitHub certifications are <strong>valid for two years</strong>; GitHub is moving to Microsoft's recertification process and extends certifications expiring before then by six months</td><td>Two years for GH-200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Study guide: "Get trained" links only the learning path's <strong>part 1</strong></td><td>Part 2 holds the custom actions, GitHub Packages and enterprise modules</td><td>Do both parts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Learn, <em>Create a custom GitHub action</em>: <code>runs.using: "node16"</code></td><td><code>node24</code>; Node 16 and Node 20 are gone from the runners</td><td>Any answer with <code>node16</code> or <code>node20</code> is outdated</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Learn samples: <code>actions/checkout@v4</code>, <code>setup-node@v4</code>, <code>actions/cache@v4</code></td><td>Current majors are <code>checkout@v7</code>, <code>setup-node@v7</code>, <code>cache@v6</code>, <code>upload-artifact@v7</code>, <code>download-artifact@v8</code></td><td>Old majors still appear in questions; recognise them</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Older material: <code>pull_request_target</code> runs the PR base branch's workflow; checkout of the PR head "just works"</td><td>Since 8 December 2025 it always runs the default branch's workflow; <code>checkout@v7</code> refuses fork PR code there without <code>allow-unsafe-pr-checkout</code></td><td>The safe answer never runs PR code with secrets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Older material: reusable workflows nest 4 deep, 20 per file; 10 dispatch inputs</td><td>10 levels and 50 workflows (November 2025); 25 inputs (December 2025)</td><td>Use the new numbers</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certification page: practice assessment</td><td>"Not currently available"</td><td>Use the exam sandbox and hands-on practice</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <h2 id="commonly-mistaken-topics">Commonly mistaken topics</h2>
  <p>Each one reads as a failed run and the run that fixed it.</p>

  <Mistake topic="Jobs with needs">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>A job with <code>needs: build</code> runs once <code>build</code> has finished.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>It runs only if <code>build</code> <strong>succeeded</strong>; otherwise it is skipped, and the skip propagates. To run anyway, add <code>if: always()</code> (or <code>{"!cancelled()"}</code>) and check <code>needs.build.result</code>.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Conditions and the implicit success()">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p><code>if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'</code> on a notification step runs on main even after a failure.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Without a status function, <code>success() &amp;&amp;</code> is added, so it is skipped after a failure. Use <code>if: failure() &amp;&amp; …</code> or <code>always() &amp;&amp; …</code>.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Boolean dispatch inputs">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p><code>if: github.event.inputs.dry-run</code> is false when the box is unticked.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p><code>github.event.inputs</code> holds strings, and <code>'false'</code> is truthy. Use <code>inputs.dry-run</code>, which keeps the boolean type.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="GITHUB_ENV in the same step">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>After <code>echo "X=1" &gt;&gt; "$GITHUB_ENV"</code>, <code>$X</code> is set in the same <code>run</code> script and in the next job.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>It is set for the <strong>following steps of the same job</strong> only. Use a shell variable within the step, and a job output to cross jobs.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Secrets on fork pull requests">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>A <code>pull_request</code> run from a fork can read repository secrets if the workflow references them.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Fork PR runs get <strong>no secrets</strong> and a <strong>read-only</strong> <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>. <code>pull_request_target</code> has secrets but runs the default branch's code — never check out and run the PR there.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Workflows triggering workflows">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>A tag pushed with <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> starts the <code>on: push: tags</code> release workflow.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Events created with <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> start no new runs, except <code>workflow_dispatch</code> and <code>repository_dispatch</code>. Use a GitHub App token, or dispatch the workflow.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Cache versus artifact">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>Use <code>actions/cache</code> to hand the build output from the build job to the deploy job.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Use an <strong>artifact</strong>: it belongs to the run and is guaranteed to be there. Caches are for dependencies, can be evicted at any time, and are scoped by branch.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Composite actions and secrets">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>A composite action can read <code>{'${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }}'}</code> directly.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Composite actions have no <code>secrets</code> context. Declare an input and have the workflow pass the secret into it.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="What a reusable workflow inherits">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>The called workflow sees the caller's <code>env</code> values and secrets automatically.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Neither. <code>env</code> is never propagated — use inputs — and secrets must be mapped or passed with <code>secrets: inherit</code>. The <code>github</code> context, by contrast, is the caller's.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Docker actions on any runner">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>A Docker container action works on <code>windows-latest</code> and <code>macos-latest</code> because Docker runs there.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Docker container actions, service containers and job containers need a <strong>Linux</strong> runner. Write a JavaScript action for cross-platform use.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Reaching a service container">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>A job running in <code>container: node:24</code> reaches its Postgres service at <code>localhost:5432</code>.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>In a container job, use the <strong>service label</strong> as the hostname (<code>postgres:5432</code>). <code>localhost</code> plus a <code>ports</code> mapping is for jobs that run directly on the runner.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Environment secrets">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>Environment secrets are available to every job in the workflow, like repository secrets.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Only a job that names the environment gets them, and only once its protection rules pass — they are read when that job starts.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Granting one permission">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>Adding <code>permissions: pull-requests: write</code> keeps the token's other default scopes.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Once any scope is listed, every unlisted scope becomes <code>none</code>. List everything the job needs — typically <code>contents: read</code> as well.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Version tags are fixed">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p><code>uses: vendor/action@v1.4.2</code> always runs the same code.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Tags can be moved or recreated. Only a <strong>full commit SHA</strong> is fixed — or a commit recorded in a workflow lockfile, or a tag protected by an immutable release.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Scheduled runs">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>A schedule runs on every branch that contains the workflow, at the runner's local time.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>Schedules run on the <strong>latest commit of the default branch</strong>, in <strong>UTC</strong> unless the entry sets a <code>timezone</code>, and may be delayed under load.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Re-running a workflow">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>Re-running picks up the latest commit and runs with the permissions of whoever clicked re-run.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>A re-run uses the <strong>same commit and ref</strong>, and the privileges of the actor who triggered the <strong>original</strong> run.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="continue-on-error and step results">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>After a failed step with <code>continue-on-error: true</code>, <code>steps.x.conclusion == 'failure'</code>.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p><code>outcome</code> is <code>failure</code>; <code>conclusion</code> is <code>success</code>. Test <code>outcome</code> to react to the real result.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="OIDC needs a stored credential">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>OIDC to Azure still needs the client secret stored as a repository secret.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p>No secret at all: a federated credential trusts GitHub's token. The job needs <code>id-token: write</code>, and the IDs can live in variables.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <Mistake topic="Stopping a workflow">
    <Fragment slot="trap"><p>Delete the workflow file to pause a noisy schedule for a week.</p></Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="fix"><p><strong>Disable</strong> it: the file and history stay, and enabling it again is one click. Deleting is a commit, and other branches with the file can still trigger it.</p></Fragment>
  </Mistake>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <h2 id="decision-trees">Decision trees</h2>
  <Mermaid code={whichReuse} caption="Template, reusable workflow or composite action." />
  <Mermaid code={whichCredential} caption="Which credential a workflow should use." />

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <h2 id="scenario-to-answer">Scenario → answer lookup</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>The scenario says…</th><th>Reach for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>"Run only when files under <code>docs/</code> change"</td><td><code>on: push: paths: ['docs/**']</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Let operators start a deploy and pick the target"</td><td><code>workflow_dispatch</code> with a <code>choice</code> or <code>environment</code> input</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"An external system should start the workflow"</td><td><code>repository_dispatch</code> with an <code>event_type</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Run the same tests on three OSes and two runtimes"</td><td><code>strategy.matrix</code>; <code>fail-fast: false</code> to see every result</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Tests need a real database"</td><td><code>services:</code> with a health check</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Avoid repeating the same <code>env</code> block in one file"</td><td>A YAML anchor and alias</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Pass a version from one job to the next"</td><td><code>$GITHUB_OUTPUT</code> → job <code>outputs</code> → <code>needs.&lt;job&gt;.outputs</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Show test results on the run page"</td><td><code>$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"A new push should cancel the PR's running build"</td><td><code>concurrency</code> with <code>cancel-in-progress: true</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Deployments must run one at a time, none dropped"</td><td><code>concurrency</code> with <code>queue: max</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Production needs a human approval"</td><td>Environment with required reviewers</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Why was this step skipped?"</td><td>Re-run with debug logging; read the <code>##[debug]</code> condition trace</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Retry only the flaky Windows leg"</td><td>Re-run that matrix job</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Every team's pipeline must follow one central definition"</td><td>Reusable workflow, plus a ruleset requiring it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Bundle three setup steps used in many jobs"</td><td>Composite action</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Share a private action with the rest of the organisation"</td><td>The action repository's Actions <strong>Access</strong> setting</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Only allow GitHub and verified-creator actions"</td><td>Allowed-actions policy at organisation or enterprise level</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Stop one compromised action everywhere, now"</td><td>A <code>!owner/repo@*</code> blocklist pattern</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Jobs fail behind the organisation's IP allow list"</td><td>Larger runners with static IPs, or self-hosted runners</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Keep production runners for the deploy workflow only"</td><td>Runner group restricted to selected workflows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Clean machine for every job on our own infrastructure"</td><td>Ephemeral self-hosted runners, autoscaled with ARC</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Which Python versions are preinstalled?"</td><td>The image README in <code>actions/runner-images</code>, linked from <strong>Set up job</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Rotate a secret from a script"</td><td>Fetch the public key, encrypt, <code>PUT …/actions/secrets/{'{name}'}</code> — or <code>gh secret set</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Issue titles end up in a <code>run:</code> script"</td><td>Pass them through <code>env:</code> and quote them</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Deploy to the cloud without long-lived keys"</td><td>OIDC: <code>id-token: write</code> and a federated credential</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Prove a binary came from our pipeline"</td><td><code>actions/attest</code>, then <code>gh attestation verify</code> before deploy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Make sure the action code never changes under us"</td><td>Full-SHA pins (enforced by policy) or a workflow lockfile</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Artifacts are filling the storage quota"</td><td>Shorter <code>retention-days</code> and a lower retention policy via the API</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <h2 id="exam-day-caveats">Exam-day caveats</h2>
  <Callout type="exam" title="Read the YAML before the question">
    <p>
      Many items are a workflow followed by "what happens?". Read <code>on:</code>, <code>permissions</code> and every
      <code>needs</code> and <code>if</code> before you read the answer options — the trap is usually a filter that does
      not match, a skipped dependency or a missing scope.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout type="warn" title="Watch for the old syntax in the options">
    <p>
      <code>::set-output</code>, <code>@v3</code> artifact actions, <code>node16</code> and
      <code>ubuntu-20.04</code> make an option wrong today, however tidy it looks.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout type="tip" title="Prefer the enforceable answer">
    <p>
      When two options both "work", the exam tends to prefer the one that is enforced by the platform — a policy, a
      protection rule, a permission, a pin — over one that relies on people remembering to do the right thing.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <h2 id="pre-exam-checklist">Pre-exam checklist</h2>
  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>I can name the event, filters and branch behind any <code>on:</code> block, including <code>schedule</code>, <code>workflow_dispatch</code> and <code>pull_request_target</code>.</li>
    <li>I can expand a matrix with <code>include</code> and <code>exclude</code>, and an anchored file, in my head.</li>
    <li>I know which contexts are available in a job <code>if</code>, <code>runs-on</code> and a step <code>if</code>.</li>
    <li>I can move a value step → step, job → job and reusable workflow → caller.</li>
    <li>I can tell a cache from an artifact, and know their scope, retention and limits.</li>
    <li>I can write <code>action.yml</code> for JavaScript, Docker and composite actions, and know where each runs.</li>
    <li>I know the Marketplace publishing requirements and why consumers pin to SHAs.</li>
    <li>I can set allowed-actions, SHA pinning, fork PR and token-permission policies, and know they only tighten downwards.</li>
    <li>I can register a self-hosted runner, put it in a group and target it with labels.</li>
    <li>I know secret and variable scopes, precedence and limits, and how the API encrypts secrets.</li>
    <li>I can spot script injection and fix it with <code>env:</code>, and explain why <code>pull_request_target</code> is dangerous.</li>
    <li>I can configure OIDC to a cloud provider and read a subject claim.</li>
    <li>I can attest an artifact and verify it before deployment.</li>
    <li>I have tried the <a href="https://ghcertdemo.starttest.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">exam sandbox</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</NoteLayout>
